        "output": "5\n3",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: The trees T1T_1T1​ and T2T_2T2​ are:\n\n\nFor u=3u = 3u=3 and v=5v=5v=5, LCA1(3,5)=3LCA_1(3,5) = 3LCA1​(3,5)=3, LCA2(3,5)=3LCA_2(3,5) = 3LCA2​(3,5)=3, H1(3)=2,H2(3)=3H_1(3) = 2,H_2(3) = 3H1​(3)=2,H2​(3)=3. Thus, H1(LCA1(3,5))+H2(LCA2(3,5))=H1(3)+H2(3)=5H_1(LCA_1(3,5)) + H_2(LCA_2(3,5)) = H_1(3) + H_2(3) = 5H1​(LCA1​(3,5))+H2​(LCA2​(3,5))=H1​(3)+H2​(3)=5.\nIt can be proven that 555 is the maximum possible answer for this case amongst all pairs (u,v)(u,v)(u,v).\n\nTest case 222: The trees T1T_1T1​ and T2T_2T2​ are:\n\n\nFor u=2u = 2u=2 and v=4v=4v=4, LCA1(2,4)=2LCA_1(2,4) = 2LCA1​(2,4)=2, LCA2(2,4)=1LCA_2(2,4) = 1LCA2​(2,4)=1, H1(2)=2,H2(1)=1H_1(2) = 2,H_2(1) = 1H1​(2)=2,H2​(1)=1. Thus, H1(LCA1(2,4))+H2(LCA2(2,4))=H1(2)+H2(1)=3H_1(LCA_1(2,4)) + H_2(LCA_2(2,4)) = H_1(2) + H_2(1) = 3H1​(LCA1​(2,4))+H2​(LCA2​(2,4))=H1​(2)+H2​(1)=3.\nIt can be proven that 333 is the maximum possible answer for this case amongst all pairs (u,v)(u,v)(u,v)."
      }
